Aggregating over a confounding variable can reverse an association: it may happen that
$
  PP(A mid(|) B inter C) & > PP(A mid(|) B inter C^cp), \
  PP(A mid(|) B^cp inter C) & > PP(A mid(|) B^cp inter C^cp),
$
yet $PP(A mid(|) C) < PP(A mid(|) C^cp)$. The reversal arises when data from disparate populations are aggregated, an effect called confounding; in the inequality chain, it is possible precisely because $PP(B mid(|) C) != PP(B mid(|) C^cp)$.
